Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  22 REMOVED_MONTH 98 ACCESSIONED ON  22 REMOVED_MONTH 98 at 14 45 CLINICAL DATA  Right breast biopsy                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 2  REMOVED_DATE CM  EXTENDING TO MULTIPLE INKED MARGINS AT BROAD FRONT  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Blood vessel and lymphatic vessel invasion is not seen  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  24 REMOVED_MONTH 98 at 16 32                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Right   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  07 MAY 98 at 14 14 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number only  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 3cm piece of firm breast tissue inked and sectioned to reveal tan white firm cut surface  A representative section is frozen as FX  1 and resubmitted in cassette A  Representative section of the rest is submitted in cassettes B through F  Resident  STEPHEN LAU  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  1   RIGHT BREAST BIOPSY   INVASIVE CARCINOMA  O R  Resident  STUART HOUSER  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Eugene F  Ruffolo  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Edwin Lee Carter  M D  PART   BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY , Pathologic_size.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  21 REMOVED_MONTH 99 ACCESSIONED ON  21 REMOVED_MONTH 99 at 11 47 CLINICAL DATA  Bilateral breast mass with FNA suggestions of lymphoma                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  DIFFUSE LARGE B CELL LYMPHOMA  SMALL FOCI  PROBABLY ARISING FROM A LOW GRADE B CELL LYMPHOMA  SEE NOTE   NOTE  This case is unusual and is difficult to subclassify precisely  There is a dense nodular and diffuse lymphoid infiltrate  forming an ill defined mass and extensively involving the surrounding fat  The nodules vary in size and appear to have a lobular arrangement  consisting of peri acinar periductal proliferation of small lymphocytes admixed with many scattered transformed large lymphocytes  Some smaller nodules are vaguely defined and are composed of dense small to medium sized lymphocytes  some with descinable pale cytoplasm  Between nodules and in fat are large non cleaved lymphocytes in clusters or occasionally in sheets with admixed medium sized or small cells  Monotonous populations that are typical of centrocytes or marginal zone cells are not seen  Definitive follicular structures are not an obvious histologic feature  On morphologic grounds  the number of large atypical lymphocytes infiltrating the tissue is consistent  at least focally  with a diagnosis of large cell lymphoma  The lymphoid proliferation with singly distributed large cell component in many areas suggests that the large cell lymphoma may be associated with a low grade lymphoma  Immunoperoxidase stains on the paraffin section show that large cells are  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     confirming the diagnosis of diffuse large B cell lymphoma  However  The B cells are  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   arguing against the presence of a follicular or mantle cell lymphoma  see addendum   Although the extranodal location  the intimate association of lymphoid infiltrate with breast lobules and lack of large cell sheeting out tend to imply a lymphoma of mucosa associated lymphoid tissue  MALT  type  the histomorphology and immunophenotype are non specific and not sufficient to definitively subclassify the low grade component  Dr  Judith A  Ferry has reviewed the case the concurs with the diagnosis  Diagnosis by  HENRY DONG  MD Signed On  01 REMOVED_MONTH 99 at 07 58                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOPEROXIDASE STAINS  FROZEN AND PARAFFIN SECTIONS  HP  REMOVED_CASE_ID    With antibodies to immunoglobulin there is a suggestion of monotypic  IgM Lambda  staining of many lymphoid cells in nodular and diffuse patterns  With antibodies to B lineage antigen s    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   there is staining of most large cells and many small to medium sized cells  The B cells are negative for antibodies to  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Large cells are positive for Bcl 2  With antibody to  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   there is staining of dendritic pattern within some small nodules  Together with the morphologic features on routine sections  these results support the diagnosis of a B cell lymphoma and argue against mantle and follicular lymphoma  See note  NOTE  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  HENRY DONG  MD Signed On  05 REMOVED_MONTH 99 at 23 18 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient 