Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  MICHELLE C SPECHT  M D  Resident  Olga Kolman  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D             Addended Report             Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results Reason for Addendum  2  Additional Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization  FISH  Results CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 58 year old woman with a history of left breast cancer who now presents with calcifications in the right breast  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISION  1  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA WITH MUCINOUS FEATURES  2  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU 3  LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU  4  ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA 5  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGE WITH MODERATE DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  USUAL TYPE AND ASSOCIATED CALCIFICATIONS  6  FIBROADENOMA  7  M NCKEBERG CALCIFIC ARTERIOSCLEROSIS   SEE TABLE 1 AND NOTE    TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS 1 INVASIVE CARCINOMA TUMOR SIZE  REMOVED_DATE CM  microscopic measurement  GRADE  2 Accurate grading is limited by the small size of the invasive carcinoma  LYMPHATIC VESSEL INVASION  Not identified  BLOOD VESSEL INVASION  Not identified  MARGIN OF INVASIVE CARCINOMA  The distances to all margins measure 0 2cm or greater  LOCATION OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Within and beyond the region of the mass  GRADE OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  2 MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Positive at the posterior margin of the specimen along a broad front  Ductal carcinoma in situ extends to   0 1cm from the inferior margin of the specimen along a broad front  STAINS FOR RECEPTORS  Requested on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   NOTE  This case was very challenging and required additional sections and multiple levels  The resection margins of the specimen were inked and microscopically evaluated  Sections of the lumpectomy specimen reveal a small focus of invasive ductal carcinoma present in association with copious extracellular mucin  The invasive carcinoma is mostly present in a 0 2cm focus on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   however a minute portion of invasive carcinoma with extravasated mucin is also noted on at least one of five level sections on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Ductal carcinoma in situ is noted in small foci both in association with the main tumor as well as in sections away from the main tumor mass  A panel of immunohistochemical stains for cytokeratin  p63  myosin and calponin were performed on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   The immunostaining pattern is limited despite repeating the stains  however  in some sections the immunoperoxidase stains highlight loss of the myoepithelial layer in areas of invasive ductal carcinoma and support the diagnosis  An immunoperoxidase stain for cytokeratin REMOVED_DATE also performed on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   which was also repeated  supports the diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in situ  An immunoperoxidase stain for e cadherin on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  supports the diagnosis of focal lobular carcinoma in situ  Dr  Fredrick Koerner has reviewed selected slides for this case and concurs with the diagnosis  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN and  right breast mass   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm portion of breast tissue with a long suture designating lateral and a short suture designating superior  The external surface of the specimen is fragmented  The long axis of the specimen is medial to lateral  and a needle localization guide wire runs from medial to lateral along the anterior aspect  The specimen is inked as follows  Anterior   blue  posterior   black  superior   green  and inferior   yellow  The specimen is sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE to 2 0cm fibrous area which corresponds to the area in which calcifications are identified in the accompanying specimen radiograph  This area extends along the length of the specimen and focally abuts all margins  Multiple small cystic spaces up to 0 1cm in greatest dimension are present within the fibrous area  The remaining breast tissue is adipose  Representative sections are submitted  SECTION CODE   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Lateral margin  radially   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Representative sections of specimen  predominantly including the fibrous area  from lateral to medial   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Medial margin  radially   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Additional representative sections sampling nearly the entire specimen  Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on Tuesday REMOVED_MONTH 31   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 07 41 23PM By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  1  QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Right  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  No invasive tumor cells are seen on the slides provided for immunohistochemistry  Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation  The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows  The tumor cells stain    10  of nuclei stained  Some tumor cells stain  1 to  10  of nuclei stained  Rare tumor cells stain    1  of nuclei stained  The tumor cells do not stain  0  of nuclei stained  HER 2 c erbB 2 quantitation  The tumor cell membrane immunoreactivity is evaluated visually and reported as follows  Do not show overexpression  0 t